window.docContents[111].resolve({"/#Fin":{"contents":"The  instance for  and  requires a proof that the provided  is smaller than the array.\nIn practice, these proofs often end up being passed to functions along with the indices.\nRather than passing an index and a proof separately, a type called  can be used to bundle up the index and the proof into a single value.\nThis can make code easier to read.\n\nThe type  represents numbers that are strictly less than .\nIn other words,  describes , , and , while  has no values at all.\nThe definition of  resembles , as a  is a structure that contains a  and a proof that it is less than :\n\n\n\nLean includes instances of  and  that allow  values to be conveniently used as numbers.\nIn other words, the output of  is , rather than something like {val := 5, isLt := _}.\n\nInstead of failing when the provided number is larger than the bound, the  instance for  returns a value modulo the bound.\nThis means that  results in  rather than a compile-time error.\n\nIn a return type, a  returned as a found index makes its connection to the data structure in which it was found more clear.\nThe  in the previous section returns an index that the caller cannot immediately use to perform lookups into the array, because the information about its validity has been lost.\nA more specific type results in a value that can be used without making the program significantly more complicated:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance","header":"8.5. Bounded Numbers","id":"/#Fin"},"/#learning-lean":{"contents":"Lean 4 itself is described in the following resources:\n\n* Theorem Proving in Lean 4 is a tutorial on writing proofs using Lean.* The Lean 4 Manual provides a detailed description of the language and its features.* How To Prove It With Lean is a Lean-based accompaniment to the well-regarded textbook How To Prove It that provides an introduction to writing paper-and-pencil mathematical proofs.* Metaprogramming in Lean 4 provides an overview of Lean's extension mechanisms, from infix operators and notations to macros, custom tactics, and full-on custom embedded languages.* Functional Programming in Lean may be interesting to readers who enjoy jokes about recursion.\n\nHowever, the best way to continue learning Lean is to start reading and writing code, consulting the documentation when you get stuck.\nAdditionally, the Lean Zulip is an excellent place to meet other Lean users, ask for help, and help others.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Next Steps","header":"9.1. Learning Lean","id":"/#learning-lean"},"/#overloading-indexing":{"contents":"Indexing notation for a collection type can be overloaded by defining an instance of the  type class.\nFor the sake of flexibility,  has four parameters:\n\n* The type of the collection* The type of the index* The type of elements that are extracted from the collection* A function that determines what counts as evidence that the index is in bounds\n\nThe element type and the evidence function are both output parameters.\n has a single method, , which takes a collection value, an index value, and evidence that the index is in bounds as arguments, and returns an element:\n\n\n\nIn the case of , these parameters are:\n\n* The collection is * Indices have type * The type of elements is * An index is in bounds if it is less than or equal to the length of the tail\n\nIn fact, the  instance can delegate directly to :\n\n\n\nWith this instance,  becomes just as convenient to use as .\nEvaluating  yields , while  leads to the compile-time error:\n\n\n\nBecause both the collection type and the index type are input parameters to the  type class, new types can be used to index into existing collections.\nThe positive number type  is a perfectly reasonable index into a , with the caveat that it cannot point at the first entry.\nThe following instance of  allows  to be used just as conveniently as  to find a list entry:\n\n\n\nIndexing can also make sense for non-numeric indices.\nFor example,  can be used to select between the fields in a point, with  corresponding to  and  corresponding to :\n\n\n\nIn this case, both Booleans are valid indices.\nBecause every possible  is in bounds, the evidence is simply the true proposition .\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Arrays and Indexing","header":"3.4.3. Overloading Indexing","id":"/#overloading-indexing"}});